WEBVTT

1
00:00:00.040 --> 00:00:03.264
Suppose you have a database that
contains the records of college students

2
00:00:03.264 --> 00:00:05.040
from all over the world.

3
00:00:05.040 --> 00:00:06.509
As part of an annual report,

4
00:00:06.509 --> 00:00:11.140
a list of all the different countries
these students belong to is required.

5
00:00:11.140 --> 00:00:14.240
It's very likely many students
will come from the same country.

6
00:00:14.240 --> 00:00:18.240
So how can you retrieve the results you're
looking for without any duplicates.

7
00:00:18.240 --> 00:00:21.540
Look no further than
a select distinct clause.

8
00:00:21.540 --> 00:00:26.182
In the next few minutes you learn how to
describe the select distinct statement and

9
00:00:26.182 --> 00:00:30.418
explain what it's used for, demonstrate
how to use it in a sequel query and

10
00:00:30.418 --> 00:00:35.281
explain how it interacts with the single
column, multiple columns and null values.

11
00:00:35.281 --> 00:00:39.964
In a few practical examples,
let's start by exploring what the select

12
00:00:39.964 --> 00:00:44.501
distinct statement is in its most
basic form, distinct as its name.

13
00:00:44.501 --> 00:00:48.140
States returns only distinct or
different values.

14
00:00:48.140 --> 00:00:51.840
In other words, it returns
the results without any duplicates.

15
00:00:51.840 --> 00:00:54.740
Let's take a closer look
at duplicate values.

16
00:00:54.740 --> 00:00:59.184
As you can imagine, columns in a table
can often contain duplicate values in

17
00:00:59.184 --> 00:01:00.861
a college student records.

18
00:01:00.861 --> 00:01:04.565
For example, the country column will
likely contain duplicate values as there

19
00:01:04.565 --> 00:01:07.440
can be many students who
are from the same country.

20
00:01:07.440 --> 00:01:09.976
Let's assume you want to find out
which countries the students in

21
00:01:09.976 --> 00:01:11.240
the college are from.

22
00:01:11.240 --> 00:01:14.210
So that you can get an understanding of
which nationalities are represented in

23
00:01:14.210 --> 00:01:15.540
the college.

24
00:01:15.540 --> 00:01:18.540
You can begin by using
a sequel select statement.

25
00:01:18.540 --> 00:01:23.076
You can write the select keyword then
country followed by the from keyword and

26
00:01:23.076 --> 00:01:24.553
the student table name.

27
00:01:24.553 --> 00:01:28.978
Running this select query gives you seven
records as the result with multiple

28
00:01:28.978 --> 00:01:30.212
duplicate records.

29
00:01:30.212 --> 00:01:34.840
In this case there are duplicate
records for Australia and the USA.

30
00:01:34.840 --> 00:01:38.550
So how can you eliminate these duplicates
and retrieve a unique set of results?

31
00:01:38.550 --> 00:01:41.240
You can use the select distinct statement.

32
00:01:41.240 --> 00:01:44.631
You can write a select
statement just like before but

33
00:01:44.631 --> 00:01:47.405
this time distinct after the word select,

34
00:01:47.405 --> 00:01:53.240
the word distinct will return all unique
values in the table with no duplicates.

35
00:01:53.240 --> 00:01:57.240
You can then write the from keyword
followed by the student table name.

36
00:01:57.240 --> 00:01:58.622
Once you run this statement,

37
00:01:58.622 --> 00:02:02.140
the country's now only appear
once in the resulting records.

38
00:02:02.140 --> 00:02:04.740
All the duplicates have been removed.

39
00:02:04.740 --> 00:02:08.720
This is how the selected stink statement
can be used to return distinct values from

40
00:02:08.720 --> 00:02:09.840
one column.

41
00:02:09.840 --> 00:02:13.640
In this case you've returned distinct
values from the country column.

42
00:02:13.640 --> 00:02:19.340
No, let's take a few moments to explore
the select distinct statement in action.

43
00:02:19.340 --> 00:02:22.521
The examples that follow focus on
the select distinct statement.

44
00:02:22.521 --> 00:02:27.540
With the use of multiple columns or when
applied to a column that has a null value.

45
00:02:27.540 --> 00:02:30.863
The student table in this example,
I want to write a query to determine

46
00:02:30.863 --> 00:02:35.140
which countries are represented by
students in different faculties.

47
00:02:35.140 --> 00:02:38.140
I can use a select distinct
statement as before.

48
00:02:38.140 --> 00:02:42.549
But this time I allowed the word faculty
before country Running this statement

49
00:02:42.549 --> 00:02:46.157
produces six records the science
faculty of students from three

50
00:02:46.157 --> 00:02:50.240
different countries,
as does the engineering faculty.

51
00:02:50.240 --> 00:02:53.154
So with this statement which
uses multiple columns,

52
00:02:53.154 --> 00:02:57.440
I've generated each unique faculty and
country combination.

53
00:02:57.440 --> 00:02:59.869
Now let's return to
the table once more and

54
00:02:59.869 --> 00:03:04.440
examine how select this thing deals
with null values and columns.

55
00:03:04.440 --> 00:03:09.040
In this example, I have a new student
named Julia Smith from the USA.

56
00:03:09.040 --> 00:03:12.440
She's not yet been assigned a faculty or
school address.

57
00:03:12.440 --> 00:03:13.166
As a result,

58
00:03:13.166 --> 00:03:17.840
both fields within these columns assigned
to Julia Smith contain a value of null.

59
00:03:17.840 --> 00:03:21.252
So let's see what happens when I run
the same select distinct statement as

60
00:03:21.252 --> 00:03:23.040
a previous example.

61
00:03:23.040 --> 00:03:25.340
How does it handle the null values?

62
00:03:25.340 --> 00:03:29.340
In other words,
what results does it return for Julia?

63
00:03:29.340 --> 00:03:33.340
I said let go and
received the same result as the last time.

64
00:03:33.340 --> 00:03:37.425
But now there's also a record for
